# The binary, and anything built for another machine. Fifty megabytes of build
# output has no business in a history: it is reproducible from the source beside
# it, and the recipe for reproducing it is in the README.
/tomoshibi
/meet-live
/dist/

# And anything built out of dev/, which is where the small tools live. One of
# them was committed as a forty-one megabyte binary in the root, beside the rule
# saying not to, because `go build` in a directory writes the binary where you
# are rather than where the source is.
/turncheck
/snicheck
/udpcheck
/ascstub

# Built client. The marker file is tracked on purpose: the embed directive needs
# this directory to exist at compile time, so without it a fresh clone cannot
# build the server at all. What to serve when nothing has been built lives in
# placeholder.html, outside this directory, where a build cannot overwrite it.
/web/dist/*
!/web/dist/.gitkeep

# Local state. The tripcode key signs the marks people wear, so it belongs
# nowhere near a repository: committing one would hand every signature it has
# ever produced to whoever reads the history.
/dev/*.db
/dev/*.db.*
/dev/*.local.yaml
/dev/tripcode.key

node_modules/

# One package manager, and its lockfile is committed. The others leave files
# that resolve to a different tree from the same manifest, which is the whole
# thing a lockfile exists to prevent.
/web/package-lock.json
/web/yarn.lock
/web/bun.lock

# Finder's own bookkeeping, which follows anybody who opens a folder on a Mac.
.DS_Store
/roster
/peekroom

# Worktrees a subagent opened. Not part of the project.
.claude/
